# ⛔ THE WITNESS THAT FAILED TO WITNESS — kept as the record of it. Plan 22 `T3`.
#
# `at` used to build its author with `hex_editor::author_on` — the CELL's stored
# height — while the server builds one from its walker, whose feet are
# `hex_mesh::ground_under`, interpolated across the cell. They agree at a cell's centre
# and on flat ground, and differ everywhere on a slope, so a script standing on a slope
# and gesturing there should have shown the change. **It does not**, and three tries at
# it are why `probe/t3/height.loft` exists:
#
#   at 1.3 0.7 / verb place   identical — that point is in the region where the two
#                             functions agree; picked by eye rather than measured
#   at 5.2 4   / verb place   identical — the WORST disagreement (0.166 wu), and
#                             `place` turns out not to read `au_y` in any way a world
#                             can see
#   at 6 4.6   / verb fence   identical — `fence` DOES read `au_y`, and still cannot
#                             see it: the control in `height.loft` shows a whole
#                             STOREY apart does not move it either, because `au_y`
#                             chooses which SURFACE you stand on and this world has one
#
# ⚠ **A WORLD KEY IS A COARSE INSTRUMENT FOR A FLOAT, AND THAT IS THE FINDING.** Every
# run above is a correct measurement of the wrong quantity: a gesture seats on integer
# height units, so two thirds of a unit is below what any saved world can carry. The
# question had to be asked of the two FUNCTIONS, in floats, which is what `height.loft`
# does — and the answer is that they differ and that nothing here can tell.
rate 0
at 0 0 30
verb raise
wait rebuilt
at 6 4.6 30
verb fence
save t3slope
